## The short version — the rules that have each cost a round

1. **Implement only this task.** Do not run `git commit`, `push`, `add`, `switch`,
   `checkout`, or `branch`. Leave changes in the working tree. Do not change the
   task's status. Do not start another task.
2. **Run `{VERIFY_CMD}` as your last action, always**, and paste its real output.
   A run with no result line is a **failure**, not an ambiguity — an expected line
   that is absent is the finding. Never claim a test passed without output showing
   it ran.
3. **Every scratch file goes in `{SCRATCH_DIR}/`, inside this worktree.** Writes to
   `/tmp`, `/var/tmp` and `$TMPDIR` are auto-rejected, and that rejection is
   terminal — the run ends there. Four rounds have died on it.
4. **Every path you write is worktree-relative, never absolute.** Two rounds died
   on one mistyped character in a long absolute path; the sandbox correctly read it
   as outside the worktree and killed the run mid-edit.
5. **If the same thing fails twice, change approach.** Do not retry a third time. If
   the write tool fails twice on one file, use a shell heredoc. A ceiling is not
   something a retry can clear — one round re-emitted the same failing call 188
   times over 25 minutes and produced nothing.
6. **A test that cannot fail is worse than no test.** Before you finish, re-read
   each test you wrote and ask what production change would make it fail. If the
   answer is "none", it is not done. No assertion-free tests, no assertions over
   literals, no `if let x { assert }` without an else.
7. **Never end a round with a question.** You run unattended; nobody reads the log
   until after you have stopped. Do every unambiguous part, take the most literal
   reading of the criteria, and state any assumption in one sentence at the end.
8. **Read the files the task names, then start editing.** Do not survey the repo and
   do not run the suite before you have written anything. If the task states a
   measurement, do not re-measure it.
9. **Do not spawn a subagent.** The task names every file and signature you need.

Finish with: what you changed, what you ran, what it printed, what you could not do.

