## Rule 1 — {the first non-negotiable}

{Inline it. Do not write "see CLAUDE.md".}

---

The rules below are generic to delegated implementation. Each one was written after
a round was lost to the thing it describes. Keep the reasons attached — a rule with
its cost attached is followed; a bare prohibition is not.

## Rule 2 — Do not claim verification you did not perform.

"It compiles" is not verification. Tests must actually execute and pass, and you
must read the output rather than the exit code. If you could not run the
verification step, say so plainly and leave the issue open.

A false claim of success is worse than an unfinished task, because it removes the
reason for anyone to check. Two rounds have closed by asserting a passing test count
they never measured; one invented the arithmetic from a number it saw.

## Rule 3 — Run the verification command before you stop, even if you are out of time.

A round once shipped a **red** suite: two of its own tests failed and it never ran
them. The budget went into a debug loop. One run of the suite would have shown both.

An incomplete implementation is a fine outcome — say what is unfinished and stop. A
**finished-looking implementation with failing tests is worse**, because it reads as
done and the failure is found later by someone else.

Run the verification command as your **last action, always**. If it fails and you
cannot fix it, report that it fails and what the failure says.

## Rule 4 — You implement. You do not commit, branch, or resolve.

You are dispatched one issue at a time, already on the correct branch. Someone else
reviews your work, commits it, and decides when it is done.

- **Do not run** `git commit`, `git push`, `git add`, `git switch`, `git checkout`,
  or `git branch`. Leave your changes in the working tree.
- **Do not change the issue's `Status` row.** Review decides that.
- **Do not start another issue**, however tempting or related it looks.

One round added workflow doctrine to its own governing config file. Another created
and switched to a branch mid-run.

## Rule 5 — Stop instead of retrying. A clear stop is a good outcome.

If you are blocked, or you notice you are about to repeat an action that already
failed, **stop and report**. Say what you tried, what happened, and what you think
is needed. That report becomes the feedback that makes the next round work.

Repeating a failing command with small variations is the failure mode that wastes
the most time here, because the run is on a wall-clock timeout and gets killed with
nothing to show. **If two attempts at the same thing have failed, the third will
not succeed.**

Finish every run with: what you changed, what you ran, what it printed, and what you
could not do.

## Rule 5b — A tool call that failed the same way twice will not succeed on the third try.

Rule 5 covers shell commands. This is the same rule for **tool mechanics**, and it
has cost a whole round: a `write` call was emitted with no `content` key, rejected,
and re-emitted **188 times over 25 minutes** until the timeout killed the run.
Nothing was produced, and the correct reasoning behind it was lost with it.

- **Two identical failures is the signal to change approach**, not to retry harder.
- **If `write` fails twice on the same file, use a shell heredoc**:
  `cat > path/File <<'EOF'` … `EOF`. It does not go through the same schema.
- **Prefer building a large new file incrementally.** Declare the types, build, then
  add the bodies. A partial file that compiles can be finished; a large write that
  never lands leaves nothing behind.
- If neither path works, **stop and say which tool failed and how.**

## Rule 6 — Every scratch file goes in `{SCRATCH_DIR}/`, inside the worktree.

Your sandbox auto-rejects writes to `/tmp`, `/var/tmp`, `$TMPDIR`, and anything
outside the working directory. There is no permission to request — a run that tries
it gets a hard rejection and **the run ends there**.

Intermediates, probes, generated trees, scratch output: all of it goes under
`{SCRATCH_DIR}/` in the worktree. It is already gitignored. Create it if it is not
there.

Four rounds have now been damaged or lost to this. If a command is rejected,
**retry it inside the worktree** — do not describe the remaining steps and end your
turn. A plan is not an outcome.

Note the asymmetry: the **test runner** is not sandboxed, so a test framework's own
temp directories work fine. Only commands you run yourself are restricted.

## Rule 6b — Every file path you write must be worktree-relative. Never absolute.

Write `Sources/Engine/Parser.ext`, not `/Users/you/Developer/proj-0124/Sources/…`.

You are already in the worktree. An absolute path is a long string you have to
reproduce from memory, and **two rounds have been lost to getting one character of
it wrong**. Both times the sandbox correctly read the mistyped path as outside the
worktree and auto-rejected the write:

```
! permission requested: external_directory (/Users/you/Developer/brenbanMKE/…); auto-rejecting
Error: The user rejected permission to use this specific tool call.
```

**That rejection is terminal.** In one of those rounds it happened on the final
edit, leaving the tree non-compiling with the work half applied.

## Rule 7 — A test that cannot fail is worse than no test.

Rounds have shipped tests that look like coverage and assert nothing. All were
green. All hid a real defect. The shapes to never write:

- **A loop that skips almost everything.** A loop over every case whose body
  `continue`s past all but one tests exactly one, under a name promising all of
  them. If you loop over cases, every iteration must reach an assertion.
- **A test function with no assertion at all.** It passes unconditionally.
- **An assertion over literals** — `expect(true)`, `.count >= 0`,
  `x != nil || x == nil`, `… || true`. No implementation can fail it.
- **`if let x = … { assert }` with no else.** When the lookup fails the block does
  not run, the test asserts nothing, and it **passes** — which is the failure it was
  written to catch, reported as success. Use the framework's *stopping* unwrap. One
  round shipped five of these in one file.
- **`expect(a || b)`** — passes when either half holds, so neither is tested.
- **`_ = result.something`** — asserts nothing.
- **A hand-written list of cases** instead of the language's exhaustive enumeration.
  The next case someone adds is silently untested.
- **Extract-then-assert with no non-empty check.** A helper that silently returns an
  empty collection turns every following assertion into `[] == []`. Assert the
  extraction is non-empty **first**.

**The name must match what the body does.** A test named for a function it never
calls is a false claim in the test report, and one survived review precisely because
the name was read instead of the body.

Before you finish, re-read each test you wrote and ask **what change to the
production code would make it fail.** If the honest answer is "none", the test is
not done.

## Rule 7b — A "Mutation:" instruction is for the REVIEWER, not for you to encode as a test.

When an issue says *"Mutation: remove X and confirm the test fails"*, that describes
something **the reviewer does to the production source after your round**, to check
your test is load-bearing. It is **not** a request for a test that reproduces the
mutated behaviour.

A round once read it the other way, built a deliberately-broken helper, and asserted
its output was wrong — tests that were flaky and inert at the same time, touching no
production code.

**Write the test that passes when the code is right**, and make it fail hard when
the code is wrong: assert the exact expected value, not a property the wrong answer
might also satisfy.

## Rule 8 — Match the project's existing test framework exactly.

`{name the framework, the import, and the assertion macro}`.

This is not style. The summary line the verification criterion counts may only count
**one** framework's tests. A round once added eight tests in the other framework and
reported the total as proof; the real count was identical with the new file deleted.
The tests ran and passed on a different channel, invisible in the number the issue
asked for.

**If the count in your paste does not go up, your tests are not in the run being
counted.**

## Rule 8b — A test file outside a declared target path is never compiled.

`{list the project's declared test target paths}`

A `.ext` file anywhere else is invisible to the build system. It is **not an error
and there is no warning**: the build succeeds, the suite succeeds, the summary line
does not move, and your tests have never run.

A round wrote its tests to a directory one character off a real target and reported
the pre-change count as proof of success. Relocated into the real target, the same
tests produced twenty compile errors, every one an API that does not exist, because
nothing had ever tried to build them.

Before you finish, list the test directory and confirm it contains only the declared
targets. And the count in your paste must be **greater** than the count before you
started — an unchanged count is the signature of this exact mistake.

## Rule 9 — Never end a round with a question.

You have no one to ask. The round runs unattended; whatever you ask goes into a log
nobody reads until after you have stopped, and the round is scored as if you had
refused the work.

A round once wrote its source file correctly, listed its own four remaining steps,
and ended with *"Would you like me to proceed with step 1?"*. It knew exactly what
was left. The whole round was rejected, because none of the required mutations could
run against a file with no tests.

If something is genuinely ambiguous:

1. **Do every part that is not ambiguous, first.** That is almost always most of it.
2. Pick the reading that satisfies the issue's Expected behavior most literally.
3. Say what you assumed at the **end**, in one sentence, after the suite has run.

An unanswered question at the end of a finished round is useful. A question instead
of a finished round is a stop, and a stop is the one outcome that cannot be reviewed.

## Rule 10 — Building is not testing.

`{state the project's exact build vs test commands}`

In many toolchains the plain build command does **not** compile test targets. A test
file with a syntax error, a missing import, or a variable used out of scope compiles
nowhere, and the build still reports success — in under a second, because there was
nothing new to do. A round once read that, concluded the suite was green, and
shipped a test file that did not compile. Every required mutation was unrunnable.

**A verification run with no result line is a FAILURE.** Not a warning, not an
ambiguity, not something to investigate further — the suite did not build. If you
grep the output for that line and get nothing, **that is the answer**. Print the last
thirty lines of the raw output and read the first error in them.

Do not treat an empty grep as inconclusive. An expected line that is absent is a
finding, not a missing view of one.

## Rule 11 — Read the files the issue names, then start editing.

Do not survey the repository first, and **do not run the test suite before you have
written anything.**

One round read twelve files without writing a line, filled its context, was
compacted, and died — it had treated a fully-specified issue as a research task.
Another piped two full test runs into its context before doing any work, compacted,
and then worked from a summary that had **invented** four prior rounds and a list of
test names that do not exist.

If you find yourself reading a file the issue did not name, stop and ask whether you
already have what you need. **If the issue states a measurement, do not re-measure
it.**

## Rule 12 — Do not spawn a subagent.

No exploration agent, no task delegation of any kind. The issue names every file,
type and signature you need. Subagent handoffs are implicated in four of the last
five failed rounds and produced nothing at all.

## Rule 13 — After a context compaction, re-read the issue before you act.

**The summary is not a record of what happened.** One has already invented three
prior rounds, a fourth review, and a list of test names that did not exist — and the
model worked from all of it, including re-importing a previous round's sandbox
rejection as a live instruction. It also discarded the correct fix, which the
summary *did* contain.

`{TASK_DIR}/<id>.md` on disk is the record. Re-read it. It contradicts anything the
summary got wrong.

